== Route ==
{{main|2026 La Vuelta Femenina, Stage 1 to Stage 7}}
Prior to the route announcement, Spanish newspaper ''[[El País]]'' reported rumours that the challenging [[Alto de l'Angliru|Angliru]] climb would be included in the race for the first time in 2026,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arribas |first=Carlos |date=2026-03-04 |title=Las ciclistas, impacientes por medirse al Angliru en la Vuelta: “Vamos a sacarnos la máscara de que las mujeres no pueden subir esos puertos” |url=https://elpais.com/deportes/ciclismo/2026-03-04/las-ciclistas-impacientes-por-medirse-al-angliru-en-la-vuelta-vamos-a-sacarnos-la-mascara-de-que-las-mujeres-no-pueden-subir-esos-puertos.html |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=El País |language=es}}</ref> following previous suggestions that the climb could be included in the race in future.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitchell |first=Mathew |date=2024-11-14 |title=Women’s Vuelta tipped to tackle Angliru in future editions by Vuelta organiser |url=https://procyclinguk.com/womens-vuelta-tipped-to-tackle-angliru-in-future-editions-by-vuelta-organiser/ |access-date=2025-03-13 |website=ProCyclingUK.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>

The route was announced in March 2026.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=9 March 2026 |title=La Vuelta Femenina 2026 by Carrefour.es: Legendary Angliru to culminate a course designed for climbers |url=https://www.lavueltafemenina.es/en/news/2026/la-vuelta-femenina-26-by-carrefour-es-legendary-angliru-to-culminate-a-course-designed-for-climbers/3615 |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=www.lavueltafemenina.es |language=en}}</ref> It comprised seven days of racing with seven stages, covering a total of {{convert|815|km|mi}} with {{convert|14,486|m|ft}} of elevation gain.<ref name=":0" />
The entire race was held in the north-west of the country, with the first four stages taking place in [[Galicia (Spain)|Galicia]], before stage 5 in [[Castile and León]]. The race finished with two summit finishes in [[Asturias]] – stage 6 finishing at the [[Les Praeres de Nava]] (a nearly {{convert|4|km|adj=on}} climb with an average gradient of 13.5%, with a maximum gradient of 27%), and stage 7 finishing at the Alto de l'Angliru (a {{convert|12.5|km|adj=on}} climb at an average gradient of 10%, with a maximum gradient of 23%).<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Frattini |first=Kirsten |date=2026-03-09 |title=2026 La Vuelta Femenina route revealed showcasing back-to-back summit finishes at Les Praeres and Alto de l'Angliru |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/womens-cycling/2026-la-vuelta-femenina-route-revealed-showcasing-back-to-back-summit-finishes-at-les-praeres-and-alto-de-langliru/ |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Mitchell |first=Mathew |date=2026-03-09 |title=Angliru set to decide La Vuelta Femenina 2026 as organisers unveil climber friendly route |url=https://procyclinguk.com/angliru-set-to-decide-la-vuelta-femenina-2026-as-organisers-unveil-climber-friendly-route/ |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=ProCyclingUK |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Overall, the route was considered to favour the climbers, owing to the inclusion of the Angliru climb, more elevation gain than the Giro d'Italia Women over a shorter distance and the absence of the [[team time trial]] that had featured in previous editions.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" />
